      "content": "To frame these counter-arguments effectively, include a dedicated section where students evaluate the \"individual choice\" and \"market neutral\" hypotheses against documented historical evidence of policy bias. This Socratic approach should encourage them to analyze the *opportunity structure*—the limited choices available to marginalized groups due to systemic barriers like discriminatory lending and opaque credit scoring. By explicitly contrasting individual agency with the overwhelming weight of historical redlining and current data on wealth accumulation, you help students understand that structural forces often predetermine the very \"decisions\" they must make.",
